Functions and Constants in Namespaces
*************************************

.[perex]
An unqualified `strlen()` inside a namespace can mean two different functions, and the code does not tell you which one. So for a whole range of fixes, PHP code style tools have to guess, and PHP itself cannot take the shortcuts that make such a call two to three times faster. Usually one line of configuration is enough to tell DressCode what your namespaces contain: the fixes then no longer rest on a guess, and the code can be written so that PHP calls functions the fast way.


One Name, Two Meanings
======================

Look at this code:

```php
namespace App;

if (is_null($user)) {
	// ...
}
```

Which function is called here? Most likely the global `is_null()` that everyone knows. But PHP decides this only at runtime: first it checks whether a function `App\is_null()` exists, and only if it does not, it uses the global one. All it takes is another file anywhere in the project that declares it:

```php
namespace App;

function is_null(mixed $value): bool
{
	return $value === null || $value === '';
}
```

and suddenly the call above does something else. This has nothing to do with the file where the call is written. It depends only on whether that other file has been loaded.

Constants behave exactly the same way: `PHP_EOL` in the namespace `App` is `App\PHP_EOL` if it exists, and the global `PHP_EOL` otherwise. Classes do not do this. `Exception` in the namespace `App` is always `App\Exception`, and the global class is written `\Exception` or imported.


A Bit of History
================

Namespaces came with PHP 5.3, and agreeing on their design was hard. The release was planned for the fall of 2008, the first alpha came out in August, and in October the PHP developers were still deciding how namespaces should actually work. That is also when the famous decision was made to replace the separator `::` with a backslash, because `::` could not be reliably told apart from a static method call. PHP 5.3.0 finally came out on June 30, 2009, nine months later than planned.

One of the hardest questions was exactly this one: what does a name mean when it has no namespace in front of it? The proposal of October 30, 2008 gave different answers for classes and for functions. Classes do not fall back to the global name, because together with autoloading that would be a performance disaster: for every `new Exception`, PHP would first try to load `App\Exception`. Functions and constants have no autoloading, so this danger did not exist. And because most of what PHP can do comes from global functions, they got the fallback to the global name. Code written before namespaces could then move into a namespace without adding a backslash in front of every `strlen()`.

It was a reasonable compromise. Its price is that the meaning of an unqualified function name is not a property of the file but of the whole running program.


Why Tools Struggle with It
==========================

A code style tool sees files, not a running program. So for every `is_null($x)` in a namespace it faces the same question you faced above, and it does not know the answer. Yet a whole range of useful fixes depend on the function being the well-known global one:

- `is_null($x)` to `$x === null`,
- `intval($x)` to `(int) $x`,
- `sizeof($a)` to `count($a)`,
- `dirname(__FILE__)` to `__DIR__`,
- `get_class()` to `self::class`,
- adding `use function count;` so that the compiler can [optimize the call |#Faster Calls When the Name Is Certain].

If the function were your own `App\is_null()`, each of these fixes would change what the program does. An honest tool must therefore treat them as risky fixes and not make them without permission. PHP CS Fixer has, for example, `is_null`, `no_alias_functions` and `dir_constant` among its risky rules, all for the same reason: risky when the function is overridden.

This is not a theoretical loophole. A well-known testing trick is built on exactly this behavior: libraries such as php-mock declare `App\time()` or `App\file_get_contents()` in a test, so that code in the namespace `App` calls a fake instead of the real function.


Let's Be Pragmatic
==================

How many functions and constants do you really have in your namespaces? Almost certainly few, often none. Functions are **not autoloaded**: someone must load the file with the function through `require` or a `files` entry in `composer.json`, and do it before the first call. That is why code moved into classes long ago and functions in namespaces remained rare. Proposals for function autoloading have come up in PHP several times, and none has become part of the language yet.

This is also a good reason not to call a namespaced function by its unqualified name: when the file with the function happens not to be loaded, PHP reports no error and quietly calls the global function of the same name, if one exists. An import `use function App\is_null;` does not load the function either, but in that situation the call ends with a clear error `Call to undefined function App\is_null()`.

So the picture is clear: the doubt affects everyone, but real functions in namespaces are rare. It is enough to list them.


Tell DressCode What Is What .[#name-resolution]
===============================================

The most common case is that your project declares no functions or constants in namespaces at all. Then a single line of configuration is enough:

```neon
nameResolution: certain
```

Now DressCode knows that every unqualified function or constant name that the file neither imports nor declares itself is global. Not "probably", but for certain.

If you do have some functions or constants, you list them in the key `namespaces`: functions under `functions` and constants under `constants`. They are written the same way as in a `use` statement, including groups in braces. Put an item with a group in quotes; otherwise NEON would read it as a map:

```neon
nameResolution: certain

namespaces:
	functions:
		- 'App\Utils\{format, parse}'
		- App\Legacy\helper
	constants:
		- 'App\{VERSION, DEBUG}'
```

DressCode then reads a function from the list as the namespaced function wherever it is called in that namespace without an import. For functions, case does not matter at all; for constants, it matters only in their own name, not in the namespace, exactly as in PHP.

The lists and `nameResolution` are two different things, and that is why each has its own key. A list is a fact: this function exists. `nameResolution` is a decision: nothing else exists there.

| `nameResolution` | what it means |
|---|---|
| `certain` | the lists are complete, and whatever is not in them is global |
| `uncertain` | the lists may be incomplete; the same as when the key is missing |

With `uncertain`, DressCode is careful: it treats an unqualified name as global, but it considers a fix that depends on this [risky |configuration#risky-fixes]. It reports such a fix as a warning, with the reason on the line below the violation, for example <code>''the namespace may declare `is_null()`''</code>, and it makes the fix only when you list the decision in `fixRisky` or run `fix --fix-risky`. That is why `uncertain` is the default: writing a decision into the configuration does not yet mean agreeing that it may change how the code behaves. As soon as you write `certain`, these fixes are no longer risky.


Every Rule Benefits
===================

What matters is *where* this knowledge is used. DressCode does not pass it to individual decisions, but to the `NameResolver` analysis, which resolves names for all rules. The built-in rules ask it, and so does [your own rule |rule-contract#Analyses]. The knowledge lives in one place, and no rule keeps its own list of exceptions.

Rules ask it two things:

- **What is what.** `isGlobalFunctionCall($node, 'is_null')` returns `true` for the global function, and `false` for `App\is_null()` from the list. With `certain`, this answer is certain; otherwise it is only an assumption.
- **How certain the answer is.** `getUnqualifiedResolution('is_null', SymbolKind::Function, $node)` tells where an unqualified name points: certainly to the global namespace (`Global`), certainly to the namespace (`Namespaced`), or to the global namespace only on the assumption that the namespace does not declare it elsewhere (`Uncertain`). A rule that asks this reports its fix as risky exactly at the occurrences with `Uncertain`, and so never in a project with `certain`.

And it also works the other way, when a rule writes a function name into the code itself. `hygiene.aliasFunctions` rewrites `sizeof()` to `count()` and writes the new name the same way as the original: `\sizeof()` to `\count()`, `sizeof()` to `count()`. But when the file imports or declares its own `count`, or when its namespace declares `count()` according to the configuration, it writes `\count()`, so that the fixed code calls the same thing as the original.

So all rules read every name the same way, and the result does not depend on which of them asks.


A New Function Does Not Slip Through
====================================

The list would be useless if it went out of date. That is why DressCode itself checks it as soon as the configuration says `nameResolution: certain`, and it reports a finding under the path of the list, `namespaces.functions` or `namespaces.constants`. It reports a function declared in a namespace that is missing from the list `namespaces.functions`, and it does the same for `const` and for `define('App\X', ...)`. It also counts a declaration inside a condition, for example a polyfill in `if (!function_exists(...))`. As soon as someone adds a function `App\Utils\slugify()` to the project, `dresscode check` points out that it belongs in the configuration:

/--pre .[terminal]
src/Utils/helpers.php
  error  12:10  Function `App\Utils\slugify()` must be listed in `namespaces.functions`.  namespaces.functions
\--

This is enough, because unlike a call, a declaration can be recognized: it is in a file that DressCode checks. Just watch out for paths excluded from the check. DressCode cannot report a function declared, for example, in tests that it skips, and if the code calls such a function without an import, add it to the list yourself. With `uncertain`, this check is silent, because there is nothing to check. With `certain`, not even `--only` turns it off: a fix that depends on complete lists must not lose the check that keeps them complete.

You do not have to write the first list by hand either. [`dresscode init` |generate-configuration] goes through all files in the project paths, finds the functions and constants declared in namespaces and writes their lists together with `nameResolution: certain`. When a file cannot be parsed, what it declares stays unknown, so `init` does not write `nameResolution: certain` and names that file in a comment.

.[note]
In the future, `dresscode fix` could write the new entry into the configuration itself, instead of only pointing it out.


Helper Functions in Tests
=========================

Tests often declare helper functions right in their own namespace, for example `App\Tests\createUser()`. There is no point in writing them into the configuration, and no point in checking them either. For such a part of the project, all you need is an [override |configuration#Different Values for Part of the Project] saying that the lists are not complete there:

```neon
nameResolution: certain

overrides:
	- paths: [tests]
	  nameResolution: uncertain
```

DressCode then reads the files in `tests` carefully: fixes that depend on a function being global are only reported there as risky, and the check for unlisted declarations is silent there. The sources in `src` still benefit from the certainty. This works because the code in `src` does not call the helper functions of the tests, so they have no effect on the meaning of names in `src`.

One exception is worth noting: the trick with a fake function mentioned [above |#Why Tools Struggle with It]. When a test declares `App\time()` so that code in the namespace `App` calls a fake, this affects calls in `src`, not in the tests. Such a function therefore belongs in the project's `namespaces.functions` list, so that the rules in `src` leave it alone.


Functions Declared by a Framework
=================================

Sometimes you write code directly in the namespace of a framework, because the framework leads you to. Symfony lets you write the service configuration in a file that starts with `namespace Symfony\Component\DependencyInjection\Loader\Configurator;`, precisely so that you can call `service()`, `param()` or `env()` without an import. Symfony declares these functions in the `vendor` folder, where DressCode does not look.

That is why DressCode knows these functions itself: when the project has the package `symfony/dependency-injection` installed, it adds them to the list `namespaces.functions` without a single line of configuration and with any standard. In the output of `dresscode config` you will find them under the line `Names`, each with the source `DressCode for symfony/dependency-injection`.

Other packages provide such lists in the key `namespaces` of their [data file |replacement-maps], and these are added to what you write yourself. But neither a package nor a preset can set the key `nameResolution`: they know the framework, not your project.


Faster Calls When the Name Is Certain
=====================================

Before PHP runs your code, it compiles it into instructions for its virtual machine, and for some functions and constants it can take a shortcut: it replaces a call with a single instruction, computes the result in advance, or calls the function without the usual overhead. Let's look at what exactly PHP does, what most often gets in its way, and how DressCode writes the code so that the shortcuts apply.


What PHP Does with Code Before Running It
-----------------------------------------

PHP does not run the source code as you see it in your editor. First it compiles it into a sequence of simple instructions called opcodes, and only then does the Zend Engine virtual machine execute them. With OPcache enabled, as is common on production servers, PHP keeps the compiled instructions in memory, so the file does not have to be compiled again on every request; before storing them, the optimizer goes through them.

A plain function call is quite a lot of work for the virtual machine. For example, `$len = foo($s);` compiles roughly to this:

```
INIT_FCALL "foo"     prepare the call
SEND_VAR $s          pass the argument
DO_ICALL             call the function
```

The machine creates a call frame on the stack, stores the arguments in it and calls the function written in C, which then checks and reads the arguments. For a function that does a lot of work, this overhead does not matter. But for a function like `strlen()`, which only reads the length stored with the string, it is many times more expensive than the work itself.

That is why PHP has shortcuts for a number of functions. And almost all of them share one condition: PHP must know at compile time which function you are calling.


When PHP Knows What You Are Calling
-----------------------------------

The PHP compiler knows roughly thirty basic functions and can replace their calls directly with an instruction. Write `\strlen($s)` and the whole call becomes a single line:

```
T1 = STRLEN $s
```

No frame and no passing of arguments. Other functions have a similar shortcut:

- `is_array()`, `is_string()`, `is_int()`, `is_null()` and the other `is_*()` become a simple type check,
- `intval()`, `strval()`, `boolval()` and `floatval()` become a cast,
- `count()`, `gettype()`, `get_class()`, `array_key_exists()`, `defined()` or `func_get_args()` become their own instructions,
- `in_array()` with an array of strings or numbers written directly in the code becomes a lookup in a hash table built in advance, so the length of the array does not matter,
- `sprintf()` with a format that uses only `%s` and `%d`, exactly one for each value the call passes, becomes a plain string concatenation (since PHP 8.4),
- `strlen('hello')` or `chr(65)` with a value written directly in the code becomes the result itself.

So why is `strlen($s)` without the backslash not enough? Because of namespaces. In a file with `namespace App;`, an unqualified `strlen()` can mean the function `App\strlen()` at runtime, if someone declares it. The compiler cannot know this in advance, so it produces a generic call that is resolved only at runtime:

```
INIT_NS_FCALL_BY_NAME "App\strlen"
SEND_VAR_EX $s
V1 = DO_FCALL_BY_NAME
```

The compiler uses the shortcut only where the name is certain: when you write it with a leading backslash as `\strlen()`, when you import it with `use function strlen;`, or when the file has no namespace at all.

How much difference does it make? Twenty million calls in a loop on PHP 8.5 with OPcache enabled and JIT disabled:

| call in a namespace | time |
|---|---|
| `strlen($s)` | 384 ms |
| `\strlen($s)` | 165 ms |
| `is_array($s)` | 473 ms |
| `\is_array($s)` | 151 ms |

With the shortcut, the call is two to three times faster. In absolute numbers, that is roughly ten to fifteen nanoseconds per call, so you will not notice the difference in a single request, but in a loop over hundreds of thousands of items you will.


What OPcache Computes in Advance
--------------------------------

The OPcache optimizer goes one step further. When it knows the values of all arguments at compile time, it calls the function right away and writes only the result into the instructions. This function:

```php
function separator(): string
{
	return \str_repeat('-', 3);
}
```

compiles to a plain `RETURN "---"`. It works through a variable too: from `$sep = ', '; return \implode($sep, ['a', 'b']);` only the finished string `"a, b"` remains. In this way, PHP 8.4 can compute the result of more than 170 functions in advance. These are functions that always return the same result for the same arguments, for example `str_repeat()`, `implode()`, `strtoupper()`, `substr()`, `trim()` or `version_compare()`.

OPcache has special handling for functions that find out what is available in PHP: `function_exists('mb_strlen')`, `extension_loaded('intl')`, `is_callable('strlen')`, `defined('PHP_EOL')` or `constant('PHP_INT_SIZE')`. When it knows the answer, it writes `true` or the value of the constant directly. For `function_exists()`, however, it does so only when the function really exists: a missing function can appear later, for example after another file is loaded, so a negative answer is left for runtime.

The same condition as above applies here too: the name of the function must be certain.


Constants Are Substituted Directly
----------------------------------

Constants defined by PHP or its extensions, such as `PHP_EOL`, `PHP_INT_MAX`, `PHP_VERSION_ID` or `E_ALL`, do not change at runtime. So the compiler writes their value directly in their place, and `'a' . \PHP_EOL` compiles to a finished string. But what matters more than the substitution itself is what it makes possible: the OPcache optimizer computes with the value and can remove a whole branch of code.

```php
if (\PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80400) {
	// code for new PHP
} else {
	// code for older PHP
}
```

On PHP 8.5, only the first branch of the whole condition remains, and the second one never gets into the instructions at all. The same happens, for example, with `if (\PHP_OS_FAMILY === 'Windows')`.

And again, the name must be certain. An unqualified `PHP_VERSION_ID` in a namespace can be `App\PHP_VERSION_ID`, [just like with functions |#One Name, Two Meanings], so PHP looks it up only at runtime and the whole condition stays in the instructions. Writing `\PHP_VERSION_ID` or `use const PHP_VERSION_ID;` solves this. The only exceptions are `true`, `false` and `null`, which PHP always substitutes.

PHP with OPcache enabled does not substitute global constants that you define yourself, because their value may differ from one request to another.


Only Where the Compiler Makes Use of It
---------------------------------------

The simplest approach would be to give every constant PHP knows a backslash or an import. That is what common tools do: they write a backslash in front of every built-in constant. But the substitution of the value alone only saves loading the constant, roughly 2.5 nanoseconds in our measurement. The real benefit comes only where the compiler computes further with the value: in a condition whose dead branch OPcache removes, or in an expression that is computed completely in advance, such as `ENT_QUOTES | ENT_HTML5`.

That is why DressCode looks at where a constant appears in the code. `PHP_VERSION_ID` in the condition `if (PHP_VERSION_ID >= 80400)` gets a `use const`, because a whole branch disappears from the instructions. `E_USER_DEPRECATED` passed to `trigger_error()` stays as it is, because there an import would only add a line to the header.

We measured this on a large open-source code base with almost three thousand files. A bare PHP constant appears in it in 627 places, but the compiler computes with its value in only 117 of them. Importing everything would add 457 lines to the headers of 136 files. DressCode adds only 82 lines to 40 files, more than five times fewer, and still catches every place where it really matters.


Calls Without a Frame
---------------------

PHP 8.4 added one more shortcut, this time for nineteen frequently used functions: `str_contains()`, `str_starts_with()`, `strpos()`, `strstr()`, `substr()`, `str_replace()`, `strtr()`, `trim()`, `implode()`, `in_array()`, `preg_match()`, `preg_replace()`, `min()`, `max()`, `class_exists()`, `property_exists()`, `dirname()`, `dechex()` and `is_numeric()`. Besides the usual form, each of them has a special variant to which the virtual machine passes the arguments directly, without a call frame. PHP calls them frameless functions.

This is the only shortcut that works even with an unqualified name in a namespace. The compiler produces both paths and chooses between them at runtime:

```
JMP_FRAMELESS "app\str_contains"               if App\str_contains() does not exist, jump down
INIT_NS_FCALL_BY_NAME "App\str_contains"       generic call
...
V1 = FRAMELESS_ICALL_2(str_contains) $s "x"    call without a frame
```

On the first pass, PHP remembers whether `App\str_contains()` exists, so every further call costs only one jump. An import adds almost nothing here.


What Turns the Shortcuts Off
----------------------------

None of the shortcuts for functions applies when a call has named or unpacked arguments. For such a call, the compiler always produces a generic call.

A named argument is written mainly for readability: `in_array($role, $roles, strict: true)` shows at first glance what `true` means. But then PHP calls the function the generic way and also has to look up the parameter by its name:

| call | time |
|---|---|
| `\in_array('c', $list, strict: true)` | 677 ms |
| `\in_array('c', $list, true)` | 499 ms |
| `\in_array('c', ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd'], true)` | 170 ms |

The second line is a call without a frame, the third one an instruction with a hash table built in advance. A named argument slows things down even more than an unqualified name: `str_contains($s, 'x')` without a backslash in a namespace takes 304 ms, `\str_contains($s, needle: 'x')` 462 ms.

Unpacked arguments, for example `\strlen(...$args)`, turn the shortcut off too, because the compiler does not know in advance how many arguments will come. And each function has the call without a frame only for a certain number of arguments: `preg_match($pattern, $s)` has it, `preg_match($pattern, $s, $matches)` does not.


Don't Expect Miracles
---------------------

These shortcuts make a single call faster by nanoseconds. But a web application spends most of its time waiting for the database, the network or the disk, so the response time of a page does not change noticeably. You will notice them in code that runs in tight loops: in parsers, in template engines, in processing large arrays, or in libraries called thousands of times per request. The good news is that they cost you nothing. DressCode writes them itself, and the code stays just as readable, only with a few more imports and with `true` instead of `strict: true`.


The Set compilerOptimizations
=============================

Keeping track of all this by hand would be tedious. That is why the [set |configuration#Standards and Sets] `compilerOptimizations` does it for you. You add it to your standard:

```neon
use:
	- compilerOptimizations
```

No standard turns the set on by itself. The set imports a function that the compiler handles specially, but only where at least one of its calls really gets faster with the arguments it has. It imports a constant where the compiler computes with it; a constant may also carry a backslash instead of an import. It writes the other global functions and constants bare, because a qualified name saves them nothing. And for a call the compiler optimizes, it rewrites named arguments to positional ones and reports unpacked ones.

The set consists of four keys of the section [`qualification` |decisions/qualification]: `function.global: bare`, `function.optimized: imported`, `constant.global: bare` and `constant.optimized: [imported, backslashed]`. If you prefer a backslash to an import, write `backslashed` for the optimized names in your configuration. The [next chapter |#How Global Names Are Written] describes this in detail.

The set does not rewrite a call that can be replaced by a language construct, for example `is_null($x)` by the comparison `$x === null`, or `intval($x)` by the cast `(int) $x`. Those are decisions of the section [`hygiene` |decisions/hygiene]: a language construct needs no shortcut, because it is always fast, whatever the namespace. The standard `nette` turns them on.

This is what a method looks like before the fix, in a project for PHP 8.4 with `nameResolution: certain`:

```neon
use:
	- compilerOptimizations
nameResolution: certain
targets:
	php: '8.4'
```

```php .[before]
namespace App;

class Access
{
	public function isAllowed(array $roles, string $role): bool
	{
		if (PHP_VERSION_ID < 80400) {  // Global constant `PHP_VERSION_ID` must be imported.
			trigger_error('Old PHP', E_USER_DEPRECATED);
		}

		return in_array($role, $roles, strict: true) && strlen($role) > 2;  // A named argument disables the compiler optimization of `in_array()`. // Global function `strlen()` must be imported.
	}
}
```

```php .[after]
namespace App;

use function strlen;
use const PHP_VERSION_ID;

class Access
{
	public function isAllowed(array $roles, string $role): bool
	{
		if (PHP_VERSION_ID < 80400) {
			trigger_error('Old PHP', E_USER_DEPRECATED);
		}

		return in_array($role, $roles, true) && strlen($role) > 2;
	}
}
```

Imports were added for `strlen()`, which PHP always optimizes, and for `PHP_VERSION_ID`, which the compiler computes with in the condition, so it can remove a whole branch. `in_array()` stayed bare: an import would help it only with an array written directly in the call, but `$roles` is a variable, and the call without a frame from PHP 8.4 works with the bare name too. The named argument `strict` was removed from it, though, because it would turn off exactly that call without a frame. And `E_USER_DEPRECATED`, which is only an argument, stayed as it is: an import would save only the loading of the constant. The [measurement above |#Only Where the Compiler Makes Use of It] shows why this is better than importing everything.

All these fixes depend on the function being a global PHP function. So if the configuration does not say `nameResolution: certain`, DressCode reports them as risky fixes and makes them only with permission. The details are in the chapter [Tell DressCode What Is What |#name-resolution].

How the names are written does not depend on the PHP version you write the code for. A function that only a newer version optimizes is qualified the same way: an older PHP simply does not use the shortcut, nothing breaks, and when you move to a newer PHP, the code is ready. DressCode rewrites a named argument only where it is already at the position of its parameter, so the meaning of the call does not change; here too it ignores the PHP version, because a positional argument is correct everywhere.


How Global Names Are Written
============================

The section [`qualification` |decisions/qualification] decides how the name of a global function or constant is written. Every name can be written in three ways, and every key of the section takes a word for one of them: `bare` is the bare name that relies on the fallback to the global name, `imported` is the name from a `use function` or `use const` statement, and `backslashed` is the name with a leading backslash.

```neon
qualification:
	function:
		global: bare
		optimized: imported
	constant:
		global: bare
		optimized: imported
```

With this configuration, global functions and constants stay bare, and only those the PHP compiler can work with get an import:

```php
namespace App;

use function count;
use function in_array;
```

At first glance this looks pointless: `count()` would work without the import too. But the point is speed: with the import, the compiler knows the function is global and can take its shortcut, as the [chapter on faster calls |#Faster Calls When the Name Is Certain] shows.

.[note]
With `optimized: imported`, the head of a file easily stretches into a long column of `use function` lines. The key [`imports.grouping.function: combined` |decisions/imports#imports.grouping.function] shortens it by writing all the functions into one statement: `use function count, in_array, strlen;`. Fewer lines also mean fewer tokens: an AI agent that reads the beginning of the file reaches the code that matters sooner, without first reading through a wall of imports. The `nette` standard writes them this way.

The keys go from general to specific:

| key | which names it decides |
|---|---|
| `function.global`, `constant.global` | global functions and constants in a namespace, except those decided by the `optimized` keys |
| `function.optimized` | functions the compiler handles specially, where it really optimizes the call with the given arguments |
| `constant.optimized` | constants the compiler computes with: in a condition or in a constant expression |
| `function.currentNamespace`, `constant.currentNamespace` | functions and constants of the namespace the file itself is in |

For some functions, `function.optimized` depends on the arguments: PHP always optimizes `strlen()`, but `dirname()`, `defined()` or `function_exists()` only with a constant argument, `sprintf()` only with a constant format that uses only `%s` and `%d`, exactly one for each value the call passes, and `in_array()` only with an array written directly in the code. A function falls under this key when the namespace calls it this way at least once, and then every use of it in that namespace is written the same way. A function that is never called this way is decided by `function.global`, because an import that speeds nothing up would only be extra code. `constant.optimized`, in turn, does not depend on the name, but on where the constant appears in the code. The value `bare` in these two keys means that the project gives up the optimization in favor of the bare name. Every name belongs to one key only: where `function.optimized` is `keep`, optimized functions stay as they are, and `function.global: bare` alone removes the backslash from `\array_map()` but leaves `\strlen()` and `\count()`.

DressCode writes an import the way the file already writes imports: into a statement that lists several of them, into an existing group use for the namespace of that name, and at the place where it belongs alphabetically, so that the decisions of the section [`imports` |decisions/imports] then have nothing to report. When the file already imports the function under another name, it uses that import. Where an import cannot be added, for example because the name is taken, it writes a leading backslash. On the other hand, it makes a name bare only where the bare name really reaches the global function, and it removes an import that nobody needs anymore.

And here we come back to namespaces. The import `use function strlen;` pins the call to the global function. If the project had its own `App\strlen()`, the import would change the behavior. So with `uncertain`, DressCode considers every change between a bare name and an import or a backslash risky. With `certain`, it knows that `App\strlen()` does not exist, and it simply adds the import. Naturally, it does not import a function from the list `namespaces.functions`.

Classes do not have this problem: a bare class name in a namespace never falls back to the global one, so for classes `class.otherNamespace` and `class.global` choose only between an import and a backslash, and none of their fixes is risky. A name without a leading backslash, for example `Model\User` with `use App\Model;`, is left alone, because it is read relative to the import or to the namespace of the file. In a file without a namespace, the backslash is always unnecessary, and `qualification.leadingBackslash` removes it.


Summary
=======

- In PHP, an unqualified function or constant name in a namespace means either the one from the namespace or the global one, and a single file cannot tell you which.
- Write `nameResolution: certain` into the configuration, plus the lists in the key `namespaces` if needed. The behavior of all rules is then unambiguous, and fixes that depended on a guess are no longer risky.
- A part of the project where the lists are not complete, for example tests with helper functions, gets `nameResolution: uncertain` in an override.
- Without this, DressCode is careful and makes such fixes only with permission in `fixRisky` or with `--fix-risky`.
- DressCode reports a new declaration in a namespace under the path `namespaces.functions`; `dresscode init` writes the first lists.
- Call a function from a namespace through `use function`. If its file is not loaded, you will find out right away.
- Before running code, PHP compiles it into instructions, and for dozens of functions and for constants it can take a shortcut: it replaces a call with an instruction, computes the result in advance, or calls the function without a frame.
- Almost all shortcuts need a certain name: `\strlen()`, `use function strlen;` or a file without a namespace. The same applies to constants such as `PHP_VERSION_ID`.
- The call without a frame from PHP 8.4 works even with an unqualified name in a namespace.
- Named and unpacked arguments turn off all shortcuts.
- The set `compilerOptimizations` writes the code so that the shortcuts apply: it qualifies optimized functions where PHP really makes them faster with the given arguments, and PHP constants only where the compiler computes with them.

{{maintitle: PHP Functions in Namespaces: Certain Names and Faster Calls}}
